Yes—use a small in-memory cache with a 1-hour TTL. It adds no dependencies and helps stay within the API quota.

```js
const weatherCache = new Map();
const CACHE_TTL = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour

async function fetchWeatherData(city) {
  const key = city.trim().toLowerCase();
  const cached = weatherCache.get(key);

  if (cached && cached.expiresAt > Date.now()) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  if (cached) weatherCache.delete(key);

  const response = await fetch(
    `/api/weather?q=${encodeURIComponent(city)}`
  );

  if (!response.ok) {
    if (response.status === 401) throw new Error('Invalid API key');
    if (response.status === 404) throw new Error('City not found');
    throw new Error(`Weather request failed: HTTP ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();

  weatherCache.set(key, {
    data,
    expiresAt: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL
  });

  return data;
}
```

This cache is fast but clears on page refresh. If persistence is needed, use `localStorage`; keep only recent entries and validate their expiration. Continue using the existing 300ms debounce, and consider retrying failed requests up to three times with exponential backoff.
